1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,039
Welcome to Inspironics Apex.

2
00:00:03,299 --> 00:00:06,314
Apex is the home page for the Inspironics platforms.

3
00:00:06,314 --> 00:00:09,734
It brings two products together behind a single front door.

4
00:00:09,994 --> 00:00:12,438
The first is the Innovation Showcase,

5
00:00:12,438 --> 00:00:16,072
a gallery of 235 intelligent infrastructure blueprints.

6
00:00:16,332 --> 00:00:19,419
The second is SlidesVault, the presentation library

7
00:00:19,419 --> 00:00:22,506
where every team finds, reads and shares its decks.

8
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:25,794
With Apex, people sign in once, see what is available,

9
00:00:25,794 --> 00:00:29,439
and open either platform in the same window, at the same address.

10
00:00:30,699 --> 00:00:35,129
Before Apex, the Innovation Showcase and SlidesVault were two separate products.

11
00:00:35,389 --> 00:00:39,670
The material itself, architecture plates and presentation decks, sat in shared

12
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:43,786
folders, where it was hard to search, hard to present, and hard to measure.

13
00:00:44,046 --> 00:00:45,298
Apex changes that.

14
00:00:45,558 --> 00:00:49,488
There is one front door, and one sign-in that opens both platforms together.

15
00:00:49,748 --> 00:00:52,608
The dashboard shows, at a glance, which platforms are ready.

16
00:00:52,868 --> 00:00:57,428
Inside, every blueprint and every deck is organized, searchable and ready to view.

17
00:00:57,688 --> 00:01:00,371
And the platforms turn that material into clear

18
00:01:00,371 --> 00:01:03,055
counts, rankings, progress figures and reports.

19
00:01:04,315 --> 00:01:06,082
Apex brings together three parts.

20
00:01:06,342 --> 00:01:08,891
The Apex front door is where everyone starts.

21
00:01:08,891 --> 00:01:11,384
It handles the sign-in, shows the platforms,

22
00:01:11,384 --> 00:01:13,707
and signs you out of all of them at once.

23
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:18,519
The Innovation Showcase presents the Inspironics blueprint library, from smart

24
00:01:18,519 --> 00:01:23,187
energy to digital twins, as an immersive gallery with a 3D city you can explore.

25
00:01:23,447 --> 00:01:26,452
SlidesVault is the knowledge hub for presentations,

26
00:01:26,452 --> 00:01:29,574
where every team searches, reads and downloads decks.

27
00:01:30,834 --> 00:01:34,772
We start at the sign-in, move to the Apex dashboard, and choose a platform.

28
00:01:35,032 --> 00:01:38,103
Inside each platform we explore, search, read and

29
00:01:38,103 --> 00:01:41,237
save material, and we explain every number we see.

30
00:01:42,497 --> 00:01:47,346
This is the Apex sign-in page. The user enters an email address and password once.

31
00:01:47,606 --> 00:01:49,715
When they press sign in, Apex checks the

32
00:01:49,715 --> 00:01:52,087
details with both platforms at the same time.

33
00:01:52,347 --> 00:01:55,725
Only when both platforms accept the user does Apex open.

34
00:01:55,725 --> 00:01:58,259
From then on, neither platform asks again.

35
00:01:58,519 --> 00:02:00,153
A session lasts twelve hours.

36
00:02:00,153 --> 00:02:04,041
The email can be remembered on the device, but the password never is.

37
00:02:05,301 --> 00:02:08,531
After sign-in, the user lands on the Apex dashboard.

38
00:02:08,531 --> 00:02:10,644
It has one card for each platform.

39
00:02:10,904 --> 00:02:13,158
Each card carries a live status badge.

40
00:02:13,158 --> 00:02:16,479
Ready means the platform is answering and can be opened.

41
00:02:16,739 --> 00:02:18,791
Starting means it is still coming up;

42
00:02:18,791 --> 00:02:23,171
the dashboard checks every two seconds and turns the badge to Ready on its own.

43
00:02:23,171 --> 00:02:25,112
Offline means nothing is answering.

44
00:02:25,372 --> 00:02:27,834
The account menu confirms who is signed in,

45
00:02:27,834 --> 00:02:30,811
and that one session covers Apex and both platforms.

46
00:02:31,071 --> 00:02:34,435
Signing out here signs the user out of everything at once.

47
00:02:34,435 --> 00:02:37,626
Clicking a card opens that platform in the same window.

48
00:02:38,886 --> 00:02:42,703
Opening the Innovation Showcase brings the user to its home page:

49
00:02:42,703 --> 00:02:45,228
the blueprint library for connected worlds.

50
00:02:45,488 --> 00:02:48,007
Four headline numbers summarize the library.

51
00:02:48,267 --> 00:02:53,122
Innovations is the total number of plates in the library, 235 today.

52
00:02:53,382 --> 00:02:57,741
Tech domains counts the different technology areas the plates cover,

53
00:02:57,741 --> 00:03:02,357
eleven in total, such as Agentic AI, Digital Twin, Edge AI and Security.

54
00:03:02,617 --> 00:03:05,176
Categories counts the kinds of plate, six in all,

55
00:03:05,176 --> 00:03:08,049
such as command decks, value frameworks and blueprints.

56
00:03:08,309 --> 00:03:10,745
And IoT-enabled counts the plates tagged as

57
00:03:10,745 --> 00:03:13,465
connected-device solutions, one hundred of them.

58
00:03:14,725 --> 00:03:16,609
Here is how those numbers come about.

59
00:03:16,869 --> 00:03:20,940
Each headline figure is simply the number of plates that match a rule:

60
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,324
all plates, the distinct technology tags,

61
00:03:23,324 --> 00:03:26,464
the distinct categories, or the plates flagged as IoT.

62
00:03:26,724 --> 00:03:30,259
With today’s library that gives 235 plates,

63
00:03:30,259 --> 00:03:34,534
11 domains, 6 categories and 100 IoT-enabled plates.

64
00:03:34,794 --> 00:03:39,149
The result is a portfolio summary that stays accurate as the library grows.

65
00:03:40,409 --> 00:03:45,281
Next is the Ecosystem Explorer, a live 3D model of the Inspironics estate.

66
00:03:45,541 --> 00:03:47,842
At its centre is the Inspironics core.

67
00:03:47,842 --> 00:03:51,293
Around it sit eight industry zones, such as smart energy,

68
00:03:51,293 --> 00:03:54,926
healthcare, hospitality and data centres, and five products.

69
00:03:55,186 --> 00:03:58,712
The tower shows the four layers of the Inspironics stack:

70
00:03:58,712 --> 00:04:03,227
sense, connect, model and decide. Users drag to orbit and scroll to zoom.

71
00:04:03,487 --> 00:04:06,096
Every zone and product is also listed as a button,

72
00:04:06,096 --> 00:04:07,975
with the number of plates behind it.

73
00:04:08,235 --> 00:04:12,325
Choosing Smart Energy, for example, filters the gallery to the 54

74
00:04:12,325 --> 00:04:16,793
plates tagged Carbon and ESG, and a strip shows which filter is active.

75
00:04:18,053 --> 00:04:21,949
Each zone in the city is linked to one tag, category or product name.

76
00:04:22,209 --> 00:04:25,630
The number shown for a zone is the count of plates that carry that link.

77
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:30,495
Smart Energy maps to Carbon and ESG, and 54 plates carry that tag.

78
00:04:30,495 --> 00:04:36,356
Data Centres maps to Analytics, with 82 plates. Healthcare maps to Security, with 9.

79
00:04:36,616 --> 00:04:40,225
So the city is a map of where the portfolio is deep, and where it is thin.

80
00:04:41,485 --> 00:04:44,426
The Innovation Gallery holds every plate as a card.

81
00:04:44,686 --> 00:04:47,062
At the top, the user can choose a category,

82
00:04:47,062 --> 00:04:49,382
type a search, and select technology tags.

83
00:04:49,642 --> 00:04:53,419
Selecting Digital Twin shows only the plates tagged Digital Twin.

84
00:04:53,419 --> 00:04:56,848
If several tags are chosen, a plate must carry all of them.

85
00:04:57,108 --> 00:05:01,782
Search covers titles, components, objectives and product names in the artwork.

86
00:05:01,782 --> 00:05:03,460
Every word typed must match.

87
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:06,527
The counter always reads shown of total, so the user

88
00:05:06,527 --> 00:05:09,334
knows how much of the library their filters include.

89
00:05:09,594 --> 00:05:14,095
Sorting by featured puts the 25 flagship plates, marked with a star, first.

90
00:05:14,355 --> 00:05:18,988
Hovering a card flips it to show the objective, key components, benefits and tags.

91
00:05:20,248 --> 00:05:22,383
Opening a card shows the plate full screen.

92
00:05:22,643 --> 00:05:24,999
Beside it, the viewer explains the plate:

93
00:05:24,999 --> 00:05:27,930
its objective, architecture, numbered process flow,

94
00:05:27,930 --> 00:05:30,688
components, and technical and business benefits.

95
00:05:30,948 --> 00:05:33,523
The toolbar zooms up to five hundred percent,

96
00:05:33,523 --> 00:05:36,727
saves the plate offline, downloads it, or shares a link.

97
00:05:36,987 --> 00:05:38,853
The arrow keys move to the next plate,

98
00:05:38,853 --> 00:05:41,554
so a presenter can walk a client through a whole story.

99
00:05:41,814 --> 00:05:44,590
The viewer also suggests up to six related plates,

100
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:48,033
ranked by how much they have in common with the one on screen.

101
00:05:49,293 --> 00:05:51,768
Related plates are ranked with a simple score.

102
00:05:52,028 --> 00:05:56,027
A plate earns three points if it is in the same category, two points for

103
00:05:56,027 --> 00:06:00,136
every technology tag it shares, and one point for every product it shares.

104
00:06:00,396 --> 00:06:04,637
In this illustrative example, a plate in the same category, with two shared

105
00:06:04,637 --> 00:06:08,991
tags and one shared product, scores three plus four plus one, which is eight.

106
00:06:10,251 --> 00:06:13,034
The Daily Spotlight features one plate each day,

107
00:06:13,034 --> 00:06:15,295
cycling through the 25 flagship plates.

108
00:06:15,555 --> 00:06:18,569
The About section adds four more counts:

109
00:06:18,569 --> 00:06:23,391
plates, domains, 173 AI-driven plates, and 55 ESG-linked plates,

110
00:06:23,391 --> 00:06:28,138
followed by six disciplines, from Agentic AI to Sustainability.

111
00:06:29,398 --> 00:06:33,693
The Showcase Copilot answers questions about the library in plain language.

112
00:06:33,953 --> 00:06:36,134
Asked how many ESG plates there are,

113
00:06:36,134 --> 00:06:39,102
it replies with the exact count from the library.

114
00:06:39,362 --> 00:06:43,392
Asked about a topic, it scores every plate by where the words appear,

115
00:06:43,392 --> 00:06:46,662
titles counting most, and returns the five best matches.

116
00:06:46,922 --> 00:06:50,199
Because the answers are built only from the library itself,

117
00:06:50,199 --> 00:06:53,197
the copilot cannot invent a plate that does not exist.

118
00:06:54,457 --> 00:06:58,687
The Monthly Innovation Report turns the library into a document for management.

119
00:06:58,947 --> 00:07:01,176
It opens with an executive summary built

120
00:07:01,176 --> 00:07:03,628
from the live totals, and eight key metrics.

121
00:07:03,888 --> 00:07:07,180
A bar chart shows the plates in each technology domain.

122
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:10,351
The longest bar is the deepest area of the portfolio.

123
00:07:10,611 --> 00:07:13,483
It then lists each category, with its number of plates,

124
00:07:13,483 --> 00:07:15,832
its share of the library, and example plates.

125
00:07:16,092 --> 00:07:19,187
The report is a snapshot of the library on the day it

126
00:07:19,187 --> 00:07:22,340
is opened, and it can be downloaded as a PDF to share.

127
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:26,055
Two simple calculations drive the report.

128
00:07:26,315 --> 00:07:29,244
A category’s share is its number of plates divided by

129
00:07:29,244 --> 00:07:32,393
the total, times one hundred, rounded to a whole percent.

130
00:07:32,653 --> 00:07:36,645
Data and Analytics has 68 of the 235 plates.

131
00:07:36,645 --> 00:07:40,364
That is 28.9, which rounds to 29 percent.

132
00:07:40,624 --> 00:07:44,569
Each domain bar is its count divided by the largest domain’s count.

133
00:07:45,829 --> 00:07:47,592
Two more areas complete the Showcase.

134
00:07:47,852 --> 00:07:51,947
The Offline library keeps any plate the user saves, so it can be opened with

135
00:07:51,947 --> 00:07:56,202
no connection, and shows how many plates are saved and how much space they use.

136
00:07:56,462 --> 00:07:59,822
Administrators have an Admin console showing the health of the

137
00:07:59,822 --> 00:08:03,236
Dropbox connection and the sync that runs every thirty minutes.

138
00:08:03,496 --> 00:08:07,527
Its overview reports active plates, the share classified,

139
00:08:07,527 --> 00:08:10,426
library size, and ESG, AI and IoT counts.

140
00:08:10,686 --> 00:08:13,676
Two activity charts show plate views and sign-ins per day

141
00:08:13,676 --> 00:08:16,928
over the last thirty days, next to the ten most viewed plates.

142
00:08:18,188 --> 00:08:23,081
Back on the Apex dashboard, the user opens SlidesVault. There is no second sign-in.

143
00:08:24,341 --> 00:08:29,158
SlidesVault opens on its home page, with a search box and four headline figures.

144
00:08:29,418 --> 00:08:33,739
Presentations is the number of decks in the library, 32 in this library.

145
00:08:33,999 --> 00:08:38,230
Available offline counts the decks this user has downloaded to this device.

146
00:08:38,490 --> 00:08:40,713
Total views adds up the views of every deck,

147
00:08:40,713 --> 00:08:43,038
here thirteen thousand three hundred and four.

148
00:08:43,298 --> 00:08:46,632
And domains shows the five business areas decks belong to:

149
00:08:46,632 --> 00:08:51,117
Engineering, Products, Business, Human Resources, and Research and Innovation.

150
00:08:51,377 --> 00:08:52,891
Shelves bring decks forward:

151
00:08:52,891 --> 00:08:56,083
continue reading, trending, recently added and most viewed.

152
00:08:56,343 --> 00:08:59,562
Browse by domain shows how many decks each area holds,

153
00:08:59,562 --> 00:09:02,124
and popular tags show the most used topics.

154
00:09:03,384 --> 00:09:05,552
The Trending Now shelf uses a trend score.

155
00:09:05,812 --> 00:09:08,042
Views in the last seven days count double,

156
00:09:08,042 --> 00:09:11,016
and the average reading time in minutes is added on top.

157
00:09:11,276 --> 00:09:14,819
In this illustrative example, forty views this week give eighty,

158
00:09:14,819 --> 00:09:18,638
plus six minutes of average reading, for a trend score of eighty-six.

159
00:09:19,898 --> 00:09:22,613
Typing in the search box suggests matching decks

160
00:09:22,613 --> 00:09:25,271
instantly, and remembers the last six searches.

161
00:09:25,531 --> 00:09:28,835
Pressing enter opens the Library with the matching decks,

162
00:09:28,835 --> 00:09:30,864
here the one deck that matches ESG.

163
00:09:31,124 --> 00:09:33,793
The Library can be sorted by recently added,

164
00:09:33,793 --> 00:09:36,220
recently updated, most viewed, or title.

165
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:41,479
Filters narrow it by domain, sub-domain, file type, offline availability and tags.

166
00:09:41,739 --> 00:09:45,502
Choosing Products, for example, shows the seven product decks,

167
00:09:45,502 --> 00:09:49,205
with sub-domains such as FleetExplorer, Insight Flow and ESG.

168
00:09:50,465 --> 00:09:54,085
Search results are ranked by points, added up for every word typed.

169
00:09:54,345 --> 00:09:56,475
A title equal to the word earns forty,

170
00:09:56,475 --> 00:09:59,222
starting with it fourteen, and containing it ten.

171
00:09:59,482 --> 00:10:02,452
The domain earns six, tags five and the summary two.

172
00:10:02,452 --> 00:10:04,279
Decks with no points are hidden.

173
00:10:04,539 --> 00:10:09,540
Searching ESG, the ESG Reporting deck earns fourteen plus ten for its title,

174
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:13,817
six for its sub-domain, five for its tag and two for its summary:

175
00:10:13,817 --> 00:10:15,133
thirty-seven points.

176
00:10:16,393 --> 00:10:20,793
Opening a deck shows it in the SlidesVault viewer, with a details panel beside it.

177
00:10:21,053 --> 00:10:24,226
It also shows an AI summary, two sentences that explain

178
00:10:24,226 --> 00:10:27,687
what the deck is about, and two to four learning objectives.

179
00:10:27,947 --> 00:10:30,335
AI confidence tells the reader how sure the

180
00:10:30,335 --> 00:10:34,889
classifier was about the deck’s domain and summary. Here it is ninety-two percent.

181
00:10:35,149 --> 00:10:37,322
Readers can bookmark a page with the B key,

182
00:10:37,322 --> 00:10:39,849
and jump back to it later from the bookmarks list.

183
00:10:40,109 --> 00:10:43,952
They can zoom, go full screen, or download the deck for offline reading.

184
00:10:44,212 --> 00:10:47,262
A view counts when a deck is open for more than three seconds.

185
00:10:48,522 --> 00:10:52,354
Each reader has a personal dashboard that greets them and tracks their reading.

186
00:10:52,614 --> 00:10:55,335
Started counts the decks they have begun, three here.

187
00:10:55,335 --> 00:10:57,441
Completed counts the decks they have read

188
00:10:57,441 --> 00:10:59,649
to at least ninety-eight percent, one here.

189
00:10:59,909 --> 00:11:02,543
Average progress is the average progress across

190
00:11:02,543 --> 00:11:05,234
the decks they have started, fifty-four percent.

191
00:11:05,234 --> 00:11:07,868
Available offline shows their downloaded decks.

192
00:11:08,128 --> 00:11:11,676
Continue reading brings back unfinished decks, most recent first.

193
00:11:12,936 --> 00:11:15,542
Reading progress is the furthest slide reached,

194
00:11:15,542 --> 00:11:18,702
divided by the total number of slides, times one hundred.

195
00:11:18,962 --> 00:11:21,719
In our session, the reader reached slide twelve of the

196
00:11:21,719 --> 00:11:25,139
twenty-three-slide Edge Inference deck, which is fifty-two percent.

197
00:11:25,399 --> 00:11:29,307
They finished the thirteen-slide ESG deck, one hundred percent,

198
00:11:29,307 --> 00:11:33,587
and read three of the thirty-two slides of an API deck, nine percent.

199
00:11:33,587 --> 00:11:38,302
The average of fifty-two, one hundred and nine rounds to fifty-four percent.

200
00:11:38,562 --> 00:11:41,709
A deck counts as completed at ninety-eight percent or more,

201
00:11:41,709 --> 00:11:45,551
and progress never goes backwards when a reader looks at earlier slides.

202
00:11:46,811 --> 00:11:49,971
The Downloads page lists every deck saved on the device.

203
00:11:50,231 --> 00:11:52,474
A storage bar shows the space used against

204
00:11:52,474 --> 00:11:54,877
the space available, and the number of files.

205
00:11:55,137 --> 00:11:58,944
Saved copies open first, so decks can be read with no connection.

206
00:11:58,944 --> 00:12:01,521
If space runs short, the oldest are cleared.

207
00:12:02,781 --> 00:12:06,837
Administrators have an Admin console that shows how the library is used.

208
00:12:07,097 --> 00:12:10,598
The first row shows total users, users active in the last day,

209
00:12:10,598 --> 00:12:13,872
users online in the last fifteen minutes, and views today.

210
00:12:14,132 --> 00:12:18,075
The second row shows the number of presentations, the storage they use,

211
00:12:18,075 --> 00:12:22,074
decks cached offline on this device, and views over the last seven days.

212
00:12:22,334 --> 00:12:24,989
The Daily Activity chart covers fourteen days:

213
00:12:24,989 --> 00:12:29,549
dates along the bottom, daily views up the side, and sign-ins as a second line.

214
00:12:29,809 --> 00:12:34,505
The User Growth chart shows the total number of users over the last twelve months.

215
00:12:34,505 --> 00:12:39,258
Because it is cumulative, it only rises, and a steeper slope means faster adoption.

216
00:12:39,518 --> 00:12:43,519
Below, the console names the most popular deck, the least viewed deck,

217
00:12:43,519 --> 00:12:46,777
and the most popular domain, all based on lifetime views.

218
00:12:48,037 --> 00:12:50,592
Content arrives through the Dropbox settings page.

219
00:12:50,852 --> 00:12:53,698
An administrator connects the company Dropbox once,

220
00:12:53,698 --> 00:12:56,433
chooses the sync folder, and runs the first sync.

221
00:12:56,693 --> 00:13:01,032
It then re-syncs every thirty minutes, and each new file gets a thumbnail,

222
00:13:01,032 --> 00:13:04,551
a slide count and, with AI on, a domain, tags and a summary.

223
00:13:04,811 --> 00:13:08,437
Files removed from Dropbox are archived rather than deleted,

224
00:13:08,437 --> 00:13:12,848
and the sync history records what was found, added, updated and archived.

225
00:13:14,108 --> 00:13:15,656
Let us bring the numbers together.

226
00:13:15,916 --> 00:13:18,494
In the Showcase, counts measure the portfolio,

227
00:13:18,494 --> 00:13:22,081
related scores connect the work, and the report shows its focus.

228
00:13:22,341 --> 00:13:25,872
In SlidesVault, trend and search scores surface the right deck,

229
00:13:25,872 --> 00:13:28,956
progress tracks each reader, and analytics show demand.

230
00:13:30,216 --> 00:13:31,758
Here is one complete journey.

231
00:13:32,018 --> 00:13:35,091
A consultant signs in to Apex once, sees that both

232
00:13:35,091 --> 00:13:38,472
platforms are ready, and opens the Innovation Showcase.

233
00:13:38,732 --> 00:13:41,248
They explore the 3D city, choose Smart Energy,

234
00:13:41,248 --> 00:13:44,038
and open a carbon blueprint to present to a client.

235
00:13:44,298 --> 00:13:46,642
Then they switch to SlidesVault, search for the

236
00:13:46,642 --> 00:13:49,086
matching deck, read it, and bookmark a key slide.

237
00:13:49,346 --> 00:13:52,220
Their progress updates, the deck’s trend score rises,

238
00:13:52,220 --> 00:13:54,444
and the admin sees it on the daily chart.

239
00:13:54,704 --> 00:13:57,477
Management can then review the monthly report and

240
00:13:57,477 --> 00:14:00,193
the usage figures to decide what to create next.

241
00:14:01,453 --> 00:14:03,221
So, what is the value of Apex?

242
00:14:03,481 --> 00:14:07,924
It gives people one front door, one sign-in and one sign-out for both platforms.

243
00:14:08,184 --> 00:14:10,937
It shows which platforms are ready before anyone clicks.

244
00:14:11,197 --> 00:14:13,612
It makes blueprints and decks easy to find,

245
00:14:13,612 --> 00:14:16,138
through filters, ranked search and a copilot.

246
00:14:16,398 --> 00:14:18,511
It helps teams present with confidence,

247
00:14:18,511 --> 00:14:21,708
with full-screen viewers, related work and offline reading.

248
00:14:21,968 --> 00:14:25,212
It measures what is used and what is in demand, automatically.

249
00:14:25,472 --> 00:14:27,967
And it keeps content where teams already work,

250
00:14:27,967 --> 00:14:30,951
in Dropbox, synced every thirty minutes and never lost.

251
00:14:32,211 --> 00:14:33,220
To summarize.

252
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:37,322
Apex takes the blueprints and decks the organization already keeps

253
00:14:37,322 --> 00:14:41,048
in Dropbox, organizes them, and puts them behind one front door.

254
00:14:41,308 --> 00:14:45,197
People explore, search, read and present from one place, while every

255
00:14:45,197 --> 00:14:49,142
view and every page read becomes a clear measure of use and interest.

256
00:14:49,402 --> 00:14:51,605
The result is faster access to knowledge,

257
00:14:51,605 --> 00:14:54,721
and better decisions about what to build and present next.

258
00:14:54,981 --> 00:14:57,952
Inspironics Apex. Two platforms, one front door.
